.th MKTEMP III 4/18/75
.sh NAME
mktemp \*- create temporary file name
.sh SYNOPSIS
.ft B
char *mktemp(s)
.br
char *s;
.ft R
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Mktemp
creates strings useful as the names of temporary files.
The argument should end in the characters `aXXXXX'.
The X's are replaced by the decimal process ID number;
the `a' or other character is incremented until no file
with the resulting name exists.
Use of the process ID as part of the name means that the chance
of accidental collision with
other processes is small.
The variable character preceding the X's
is useful when several files are to be created by the
same process.
For example, the call
.s3
	mktemp("/tmp/pcsaXXXXX");
.s3
when used by process
12345 returns strings in the sequence
`/tmp/pcsa12345', `/tmp/pcsb12345', ....
.s3
.it Mktemp
returns its argument after altering the characters
it points to.
Thus it is unwise to use
a pointer to the same string in successive calls.
